  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh clouds are moving in as an area of low pressure deepens in\nthe Southern Great Lakes region. Water vapor imagery shows a \nhealthy baroclinic leaf expanding across the Ohio River Valley \nalong with deep convection around the center of the low in \ncentral Illinois could signify that the low is deepening a \nlittle faster than models show. If it is , it likely wont affect\nour forecast too much outside of some heavier rain tonight into\ntomorrow. Cold air still lags behind the heavier precipitation \nwith 850 mb temperatures not falling to -2C to -3C until late \ntomorrow afternoon into the early evening. That is when the \ntransition to snow would begin from higher elevations and \neventually to valley floors by Friday morning. There could be a \nfew higher elevated areas above 2000 feet in the Catskills that \nsee 4 or more inches of snow. If the cold air can filter in \nfaster or precipitation lingers longer, then higher \naccumulations could occur at lower elevations and an advisory \nmay be needed, but too much uncertainty on the west side of the\nCatskills right now. Higher elevations of NEPA may also see \nsome locally higher amounts, especially in Wayne and Pike county\nthough the Wyoming Valley will see mostly rain and a brief \ntransition to some wet flakes before the precipitation ends. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nBehind the departing low, 850 mb temperatures fall below -5C, \nand with warm lakes, lake effect precipitation will likely form \nand linger Friday into Saturday. Forecast soundings near the \nlakes and at lower elevations like the I-90 corridor may be too \nwarm so a rain or rain snow mix is more likely. A brief period \nof snow is possible overnight at lower elevations though \naccumulations would be limited to elevated surfaces. In the \nhills south of the Thruway and in the Tug Hill, above 1000 feet \nor so, precipitation Friday into Saturday looks to be \npredominantly snow. Given the early season and instability both \nover the lake and warm ground, organized lake effect bands dont \nlook likely so widespread on and off showers are expected. This \nwill keep snow accumulations limited to a slushy half inch to \ninch on mainly elevated surfaces. Roads may become briefly \ncovered if there is a heavy enough snow shower to pass through. \nThese snow showers will struggle to make it much south of the \nSouthern Tier. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nThe pattern Tuesday and onwards looks to take on the same \npattern that we have been seeing the last few weeks. Upper level\nridging with high pressure centered over the Southeast US \nkeeping off shore flow and preventing much moisture return. \nSkies clear out with more sun but with the much shorter days and\nlow angle, it is going to be hard to get back up into the 60s \nbut could happen towards late week.",
